{"name":"Saltpan","hex":"#eef3e5","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"saltpan","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative spelling of salt pan."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/saltpan"}],"entries":[{"title":"Salt flat","blocks":[{"text":"Natural salt flats or salt pans are flat, open expanses of ground covered with salt and other minerals, usually shining white under the sun. They are found in deserts and are natural formations."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/20170809_Bolivia_1521_Uyuni_sRGB_%2837926739616%29.jpg/330px-20170809_Bolivia_1521_Uyuni_sRGB_%2837926739616%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salt_flat","wikidata":"Q935277"},{"title":"Sabkha","blocks":[{"text":"A sabkha is a predominantly coastal, supratidal mudflat or sandflat in which evaporite-saline minerals accumulate as the result of a semiarid to arid climate. Sabkhas are gradational between land and intertidal zone within restricted coastal plains just above normal high-tide level. Within a sabkha, evaporite-saline mineral sediments typically accumulate below the surface of mudflats or sandflats. Evaporite-saline minerals, tidal-flood, and aeolian deposits characterize many sabkhas found along modern coastlines. The accepted type locality for a sabkha is at the southern coast of the Persian Gulf, in the United Arab Emirates. Evidence of clastic sabkhas are found in the geological record of many areas, including the UK and Ireland. Sabkha is a phonetic transliteration of the Arabic root s b ḵ used when talking about any form of salt flat, including salt marshes and salt swamps. A sabkha is also known as a sabkhah, sebkha, or coastal sabkha."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/Sebkha_El_Melah_-_2001.jpg/330px-Sebkha_El_Melah_-_2001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sabkha","wikidata":"Q637600"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Saltph26.jpg/500px-Saltph26.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Saltph26.jpg/500px-Saltph26.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Bonneville Salt Flats, Utah ","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASaltph26.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Cono_de_Arita_en_el_Salar_de_Arizaro.jpg/500px-Cono_de_Arita_en_el_Salar_de_Arizaro.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Cono_de_Arita_en_el_Salar_de_Arizaro.jpg/500px-Cono_de_Arita_en_el_Salar_de_Arizaro.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cono de Arita in Salar de Arizaro, Argentina","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACono_de_Arita_en_el_Salar_de_Arizaro.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Sebkha_El_Melah_-_1987.jpg/500px-Sebkha_El_Melah_-_1987.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Sebkha_El_Melah_-_1987.jpg/500px-Sebkha_El_Melah_-_1987.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sebkhat El Melah in 1987, flooded. Landsat 5 image.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASebkha_El_Melah_-_1987.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Rub%27_al_Khali_%28Arabian_Empty_Quarter%29_sand_dunes_imaged_by_Terra_%28EOS_AM-1%29.jpg/500px-Rub%27_al_Khali_%28Arabian_Empty_Quarter%29_sand_dunes_imaged_by_Terra_%28EOS_AM-1%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.or